description:
Retail returns a playback SPEED alongside the locomotion clip id and switches wlk -> run at a normalized ratio of 0.5. We return a clip id only and always play it at 1x, so there is no 'jog' gait and feet slide whenever actual speed diverges from the clip's authored speed (haste, slow, mounts, movement-speed gear, bind).

Ours: ffxi-actor/src/actor_state.rs movement_animation picks wlk?/run? from a bare boolean; kuluu-render/src/ffxi_actor_render.rs infers_walk_gait sets that boolean from an absolute WALK_RUN_BOUNDARY = 3.0 y/s for remotes and from the WalkMode toggle for self. kuluu-render/src/ffxi_actor_render.rs advance_actor_pose leaves loop_duration None for every locomotion clip, so ffxi-actor/src/animation.rs always computes scaling_factor == 1.0.

EVIDENCE (tier 2, XIClient only -- no retail observation):
research/XIClient/src/XIClient/source/World/Actor/SkeletalMeshActor.cpp SkeletalMeshActor::SelectMotionResId, SkeletalMeshActor::GetMoveVectorRatio, SkeletalMeshActor::DefaultMove;
research/XIClient/src/XIClient/source/World/Actor/ControllableActor.cpp ControllableActor::AdjustAnalogKeyLength;
research/XIClient/src/XIClient/source/World/Actor/BaseActor.cpp BaseActor::GetWalkSpeed;
research/XIClient/src/XIClient/source/World/Actor/ActorTelemetry.cpp ActorTelemetry::MotionSetChg.
Full rule in notes.

scheduler_runtime.rs:780-789 (dispatch_action_started) and :1360-1363 (load_emote_scheduler):
  let Ok(root) = ffxi_dat::DatRoot::from_env_or_default() else { continue };
  let Ok(bytes) = std::fs::read(loc.path_under(root.root())) else { continue };
  let (schedulers, assets) = parse_action_bytes(&bytes);

A synchronous DAT resolve + fs::read + parse_action_bytes — which decodes every Img chunk in the file — inline in a Bevy Update system, once per ActionStarted event, with no cache. The same spell cast twice re-reads and re-decodes. In a party fight this fires several times per second.

This is precisely the hitch the file's own comment at :558 says the global effect dir was moved off-thread to avoid, and it matches the documented actor-load jank root cause.

Fix: reuse the AsyncComputeTaskPool pattern already in load_global_effect_dir, plus an LRU keyed by file_id.
